# ============================ ACCOUNTS 5 ============================
LESSONS_A5 = [
L("Chapter 1 — The silent partner: custody", "11", "<p>The tax accounts have appeared one at a time — output VAT at the invoice, input VAT and WHT at the bill, PAYE at the payroll journal. This module consolidates them into what they jointly are: <b>the state's wallet inside the business's chart</b> — and the discipline of a business that holds another party's money professionally.</p><p><b>The custody idea, stated once for everything.</b> Tax money in a trading business is overwhelmingly money the business <b>holds</b>, not money it owes from its own pocket:</p><p>• The VAT charged on every invoice was collected <b>from</b> customers <b>for</b> the state.<br>• The WHT deducted from a supplier's payment is the supplier's tax, held in transit.<br>• PAYE is the employees' income tax, withheld at source.</p><p>The grammar makes it visible — these are all credits to liability accounts (module 1's ₦7,500 VAT line) — and the operational consequence is the module's spine: <b>custody money is not revenue, not cash flow, and not available.</b> A business that treats its VAT account as working capital is spending someone else's money, and remittance day arrives as a crisis exactly as often as that habit holds. Chapter 7's reserve discipline is the cure; this chapter's job is that the disease is understood as what it is.</p><p><b>The wallet's compartments.</b> The custody and near-custody accounts a trading business carries:</p><p>• <b>VAT Payable</b> (the output custody) and <b>Input VAT</b> (the claimable asset that offsets it) — Chapter 3 joins them.<br>• <b>WHT Payable</b> (custody as deducting agent) and <b>WHT Receivable</b> (the mirror — the business's own tax prepaid through customers' deductions, an asset with a chase attached) — Chapter 4.<br>• <b>The payroll custodies</b> — PAYE, pension, and the statutory family — Chapter 5.<br>• Beside them, the business's <b>own</b> tax liabilities proper — companies income tax and its kin, provision-style accounts the accountant owns. Distinguished exactly because they are the business's own cost, not custody.</p><p>Every compartment is an account (module 1's types), fed by documents and templates (the posting map), reconciled to a register, and cleared by a remittance routine. One pattern, five appearances — which is why one module can hold them all.</p><p><b>Compliance as rhythm, not emergency.</b> Nigerian tax compliance for a documented trading business is a <b>calendar</b>: monthly returns and remittances on fixed days, annual filings on fixed horizons, each obligation with an owner and an evidence file. The module builds toward that boredom deliberately — a compliance function that is boring is a compliance function that is working. Chapters 2-5 cover the taxes; Chapters 6-8 the routine, the calendar, and the audit posture; Chapters 9-10 the case and the review.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — The Nigerian frame", "12", "<p>The machinery is universal; the landscape is Nigerian. This chapter is the map at working altitude: which taxes touch a trading business, who collects them, and what the 2025 reform reshaped. It is deliberately conceptual — current rates and thresholds are the accountant's living knowledge, not a manual's frozen table.</p><p><b>The taxes that touch a trading business.</b></p><p>• <b>VAT</b> — the consumption tax on supplies, collected and remitted monthly (Chapter 3).<br>• <b>WHT</b> — the withholding mechanism riding specified payment types, in both directions (Chapter 4).<br>• <b>PAYE</b> — employees' income tax, withheld monthly and remitted to the <b>state</b> authority of each employee's residence. This is the federal/state split worth memorising early: VAT and companies income tax run federal; PAYE runs to states — one business, several authorities, one calendar.<br>• <b>Companies Income Tax (CIT)</b> — the business's own annual tax on profits, provisioned through the year; the accountant's core territory.<br>• <b>The statutory family around payroll</b> — pension, housing, insurance, and training levies (Chapter 5).</p><p>Each has its authority, its rhythm, and its account — and the working skill is knowing <b>which machinery serves each</b>, with the computation itself belonging to the accountant and the current statute.</p><p><b>The 2025 reform.</b> The Nigeria Tax Act 2025 and its companion acts consolidated a scattered statute book and restructured administration. The working consequences to hold:</p><p>1. <b>The authority landscape was reorganised</b> — the federal service restructured under new legislation, with registrations and filing rails updated accordingly.<br>2. <b>Thresholds and reliefs were reshaped</b> — small-company regimes, rate structures, and exemption bands moved. The accountant re-reads eligibility annually; the system's contribution is that it can <b>apply</b> whatever the current answer is: templates adjustable, categories flaggable.<br>3. <b>The compliance infrastructure moved further digital</b> — e-filing as the norm, e-invoicing and fiscalisation advancing. The consequence that matters most for this module: the invoice register and the filed return increasingly reconcile <b>automatically on the authority's side</b>. Register-to-account discipline has moved from good practice to survival — a business whose returns restate its ledger matches by construction; one that files parallel computations gets the mismatch letters.</p><p><b>The competence boundary.</b> This module teaches the machinery: the accounts, the registers, the routines, the evidence. Tax <b>positions</b> — what qualifies, what rate applies, what relief is claimable — belong to the accountant and the tax adviser. Knowing where that line sits is itself a professional skill, and Chapter 8 shows it at its highest-stakes moment: the audit.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — VAT: both sides, one position", "13", "<p>Output and input, joined into the monthly VAT position, and the routine that files it.</p><p><b>The two sides in one breath.</b> <b>Output:</b> every taxable sale charges VAT through the template — CR VAT Payable, the custody growing with trade, the invoice register its document-level mirror. <b>Input:</b> VAT paid on qualifying purchases — DR Input VAT, an asset, the claim's evidence being the supplier's proper invoice. That evidence rule is why invoice quality is a money issue: a ₦100 price with claimable input beats a ₦99 price without it. The chart carries both compartments; the documents feed both automatically; the monthly work is joining them.</p><p><b>The net position.</b> The month's arithmetic: <b>output charged − allowable input = the position.</b></p><p>• <b>Payable</b> when output exceeds input — the normal trading month: remit the net.<br>• <b>Carry-forward credit</b> when input exceeds output — the heavy-buying month, the import-stocking quarter: the credit rides to offset future output. Refund mechanics exist in principle; carry-forward is the practical road.</p><p>The system computes the position because the documents fed it; the return states it; and the reconciliation <b>before</b> filing is the routine's heart: the invoice register's output total ties to the VAT Payable account's movement, and the purchase register's input total ties to the Input VAT account's movement. Two register-to-account bridges, run as one pre-filing checklist line — because a return filed from numbers the ledger cannot reproduce is Chapter 10's pattern wearing a stamp.</p><p><b>The classification edges.</b> Where VAT literacy earns its keep — the categories the templates must express:</p><p>• <b>Standard-rated</b> — the default trading reality.<br>• <b>Zero-rated</b> — taxable at zero. The distinction matters because zero-rated supplies still belong in the return and preserve input claims on their costs.<br>• <b>Exempt</b> — outside the charge, with the subtle consequence the accountant will care about: input VAT attributable to exempt supplies is generally <b>not claimable</b>.</p><p>A mixed-supply business — the pharmacy whose basic-medicine lines ride an exemption while its cosmetics shelf is standard-rated — therefore needs its <b>items tax-categorised correctly at the master level</b>, so the registers can tell the streams apart and the accountant can run the apportionment with clean data. Get the item masters right once, and the classification takes care of itself at every sale; get them wrong, and every return inherits the error.</p><p><b>Practical rules.</b> The pre-filing sequence is fixed: close both registers, tie both bridges to the kobo, prepare the return from the system, and only then file. And the carry-forward months are stated plainly on the position — a credit month is normal trading arithmetic, not a problem to disguise.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — WHT both directions: agent and sufferer", "13", "<p>Withholding tax runs in both directions at once, and each direction has a different accounting life. This is the mechanism businesses most often hold wrong.</p><p><b>As agent: deducting from suppliers.</b> Qualifying payments — services, rent, professional fees, contracts; the types are set by law, the current rates are the accountant's living table — are settled net, with the deduction credited to <b>WHT Payable</b>: the supplier's tax, held. The worked shape: the ₦1,000,000 professional invoice settles as ₦950,000 cash plus ₦50,000 custody. The agent's obligations complete the picture:</p><p>1. The custody is <b>remitted monthly</b> by the calendar, the account zeroing after (Chapter 6).<br>2. The <b>certificates are procured and delivered</b> to the suppliers whose money was withheld — the certificate is the supplier's evidence for <b>their</b> tax credit. An agent who deducts but never delivers certificates quietly injures every supplier it pays, while accumulating its own administrative debt.<br>3. The <b>deduction-detail register</b> — who, what, when, how much — serves as both the remittance schedule's source and the certificate run's checklist. One register, two jobs.</p><p><b>As sufferer: customers deducting from you.</b> The mirror, and the direction businesses neglect at real cost. Corporate and government customers deduct WHT from <b>their</b> payments — the ₦1,000,000 receivable settling as ₦950,000 cash plus a ₦50,000 deduction. The accounting: the deduction is <b>not</b> a discount, a loss, or a mystery short-payment — it is the business's <b>own income tax, prepaid</b>: DR WHT Receivable (an asset) in the settlement entry, with the customer's certificate as the asset's evidence. Two disciplines make the asset real:</p><p>• <b>Capture</b> — every deduction recognised as WHT at allocation: the short-payment coded correctly, the asset built transaction by transaction.<br>• <b>The certificate chase</b> — the standing worklist: deductions taken, certificates outstanding, aged per customer, chased with the same professionalism as any receivable. An uncertificated deduction is money in limbo: suffered but unprovable.</p><p><b>The asset's destiny.</b> WHT suffered offsets the business's income tax liability — the credit the accountant applies at filing. A business that captures and certificates its deductions pays its CIT net of what it already paid through customers; one that doesn't pays its own tax <b>twice</b>, quietly, year after year. Chapter 9 prices this at HealthTrade: the certificate chase is the module's most reliably self-funding routine.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Agent side: deduct, remit monthly, deliver certificates, keep the detail register. Sufferer side: capture every deduction as the prepaid asset, chase the certificates as an aged worklist, and hand the credits to the accountant at filing.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — PAYE and the payroll custodies", "12", "<p>The payroll journal credited a row of custody accounts in one line; this chapter opens the row — the employee-money custodies, their rhythms, and the tie that keeps payroll defensible.</p><p><b>The custody family.</b> The monthly payroll carves an employee's gross into destinations, and each destination is an account with an authority behind it:</p><p>• <b>PAYE</b> — the employee's income tax, withheld under the current law's bands and reliefs (the 2025 reform reshaped the bands and the low-income threshold — the accountant's living table; the system's contribution is a payroll setup that applies whatever the current structure is). Remitted monthly <b>to the state of each employee's residence</b>: a Lagos head office with Ogun-resident staff files in both states, so the payroll register must split by state to serve it.<br>• <b>Pension</b> — the contributory scheme's employee and employer portions, remitted to each employee's chosen fund administrator. Plural administrators, one schedule: the register splits by PFA.<br>• <b>The levy family</b> — housing fund, insurance trust, industrial training — applicability confirmed by the accountant against headcount and sector, each a small account with its own rhythm.</p><p>The distinction the grammar makes automatic, held explicitly: <b>withheld-from-employee is custody; borne-by-employer is cost.</b> The employer's own pension share and the levies the business bears are expense, not custody.</p><p><b>The payroll tie.</b> The register-to-account bridge, payroll edition: the payroll register's column totals (gross, PAYE, pension by portion, net pay) tie to the payroll journal's lines, which tie to the custody accounts' movements, which zero at remittance. One chain, checked monthly in minutes — and the chain's health is what makes every payroll question answerable: the employee disputing a deduction (the register's row, the payslip's mirror), the authority querying a remittance (the schedule, the receipt), the auditor sampling a month (the chain, walked end to end).</p><p><b>The payslip.</b> Every employee receives the statement of their own carving — gross, deductions itemised, net — every month. The payslip is the employee's certificate (the WHT logic, personal edition) and the business's first defence in any deduction dispute. It is not optional and not annual.</p><p><b>The rhythms.</b> Monthly: PAYE to each relevant state by its day; pension within days of paying salaries — the <b>tightest window in the family</b>, statutorily pinned to the payment of wages rather than the month's end. Annually: the employer's PAYE returns in the new year's first month, reduced to an extract when the register lived in the system all year — and an archaeology project when it lived in a spreadsheet beside it (Chapter 10's pattern).</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — The filing and remittance routine: the zeroing invariant", "13", "<p>Every custody chapter ended at the same machinery. Here it is once, generalised: the monthly routine that turns tax from anxiety into procedure, and the invariant that proves each cycle clean.</p><p><b>The six steps, for any custody.</b></p><p>1. <b>Close the register</b> — the month's document-level detail (invoices for VAT, deductions for WHT, the payroll register for PAYE), cut off at the period. The register is the return's evidence and the account's mirror.<br>2. <b>Reconcile register to account</b> — the bridge, to the kobo, divergences worked <b>now</b>. A register-account disagreement found before filing is a bookkeeping task; found after, it is an amended return, an authority conversation, or both.<br>3. <b>Prepare the return from the system</b> — the return restates the ledger. The parallel spreadsheet computation is the pattern this module keeps convicting: two computations guarantee a divergence, and the filed one wins the argument with the authority while the booked one wins it with the auditor. One source, or two problems.<br>4. <b>File by the date</b> — the calendar's line (Chapter 7), owned by name.<br>5. <b>Remit</b> — the payment entry, from the reserve (Chapter 7), referenced to the filing.<br>6. <b>Verify the zero</b> — the invariant, below.</p><p><b>The zeroing invariant.</b> After a clean cycle, each custody account returns to its explainable level — zero for the fully-remitted custodies, the stated carry-forward for VAT credit months. Any unexplained residue is next month's first investigation, because a custody account that will not zero is telling one of a short list of stories:</p><p>• A register-account divergence — the bridge skipped.<br>• A remittance short or misposted — the payment against the wrong account.<br>• A prior month's residue compounding — the drift that becomes archaeology.<br>• Custody spent — Chapter 1's disease, surfacing as arithmetic.</p><p>The invariant is the routine's self-test: it costs one glance and it catches all four.</p><p><b>The evidence file.</b> Each cycle files its bundle: the register extract, the reconciliation, the return as filed, the filing acknowledgment, the remittance evidence, the zero-verification. <b>One folder per tax per month</b> — boring and complete. This is Chapter 8's audit posture being built monthly as a by-product: the routine that runs well is the routine that evidences well. Its absence is why authority queries paralyse unprepared businesses — the answer exists, but assembling it takes a week, and a week's silence reads as evasion.</p><p>Practical rule: the six steps run in order, per custody, on the calendar's days. Steps 2 and 6 — the bridge and the zero — are the two that keep the rest honest, and they are the two that businesses under pressure skip first.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — The compliance calendar and the reserve", "12", "<p>The routines need their <b>when</b> and their <b>money</b>. The consolidated calendar, and the reserve discipline that makes remittance day a transfer instead of a crisis.</p><p><b>The calendar.</b> The monthly spine — the specific statutory days are the accountant's confirmation; the shape is universal:</p><p>• <b>VAT</b> — return and remittance in the month's third week, for the prior month.<br>• <b>WHT</b> — remittance on its earlier track; the agent's custody moves faster, with state-collected streams on their own days.<br>• <b>PAYE</b> — to each relevant state by its mid-month day.<br>• <b>Pension</b> — within days of paying salaries: the tightest window in the family, pinned to the wage payment itself.</p><p>The annual layer rides above: CIT filing on the business's filing horizon months after year-end; the annual PAYE employer returns in the new year's first month; the levy family's annual cycles. The calendar is built <b>once</b> per business — every obligation, its authority, its day, its <b>owner by name</b>, its evidence-file location — and it lives on the policy page, reviewed annually and at every legal change.</p><p><b>The reserve discipline.</b> Chapter 1 named the disease (custody spent); here is the structural cure: the tax custodies segregated <b>in cash</b>, not just in accounts. A <b>tax-reserve bank account</b> — module 3's purpose-per-account rule, now with its most important tenant — receives, on a rhythm, the cash matching the custody balances:</p><p>• The month's VAT position swept as it accrues, or at close.<br>• WHT deductions moved when withheld.<br>• The payroll custodies parked at the payroll run.</p><p>The mechanics are one contra entry per sweep. The discipline is the rule: <b>the reserve is not working capital</b> — it is the state's money sitting in a room with the state's name on it. The payoff is the module's promised boredom: remittance day is a transfer from a funded account, executed by the calendar, with no scramble, no bridging loan, and no conversation about which supplier waits so the state can be paid.</p><p><b>The sizing honesty.</b> Businesses under genuine cash strain resist the sweep — <i>we need that float</i>. The answer is honest, not soft: the custody money was never available, so the strain is real and must be solved with the real tools — collections acceleration, stock discipline, supplier terms, and if necessary actual credit at actual credit prices. Penalties and interest on unremitted custody are the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default. Chapter 9 shows this exact conversation at HealthTrade, and the alternative that worked.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Audits and the evidence posture", "12", "<p>Sooner or later the authority asks questions — the desk review, the query letter, the field audit. The preparation is not lawyering; it is a <b>posture</b>, built monthly.</p><p><b>The posture in one sentence.</b> Registers tied to accounts tied to documents, filed on time, evidenced in folders. When that chain exists, every authority question is an extraction exercise; when it doesn't, every question is a reconstruction project — and reconstruction under deadline is where businesses concede assessments they never owed. The posture is Chapter 6's evidence file compounding: twelve monthly folders per tax per year, each self-contained, and the audit-support task collapsing to <i>which months, which taxes — here</i>.</p><p><b>The encounter types.</b></p><p>• <b>The desk review</b> — the authority's routine reconciliation of your filings against their expectations, increasingly automated as the e-invoicing rails mature (Chapter 2's point: they now run half the reconciliation themselves). The business whose register fed its returns matches by construction; the mismatch letters go to the businesses that filed parallel computations.<br>• <b>The query letter</b> — specific questions on specific periods. Answered <b>on record</b>: the reply in writing, the evidence attached, the file noting what was sent and when. Within the stated window, factually, and without volunteering adjacent essays — answer what was asked, completely; the accountant reviews before it goes.<br>• <b>The field audit</b> — the authority in the building. Preparation is the standing posture plus logistics: a room, and a <b>liaison</b> — one person through whom all requests flow, so the audit gets consistent, logged responses instead of hallway answers. The role division is precise: the system's job is to <b>produce evidence, not arguments</b> — records extracted cleanly, drill-downs demonstrated where they help — while positions are argued by the accountant and the tax adviser.</p><p><b>The common findings, pre-empted.</b> What trading-business audits actually find, each one a chapter of this track running in reverse: output VAT on invoices absent from returns (the register-return divergence — the bridge skipped); input VAT claimed on defective invoices (the evidence rule ignored); WHT deducted but remitted late or not at all (custody spent); PAYE state splits improvised (the register that couldn't split); and payroll journals that tie to no register at all. A business that runs Chapters 3-7 has pre-empted the list — which is the point of running them.</p><p><b>Summary.</b> Build the folders monthly; answer queries on record and within the window; route field audits through one liaison; produce evidence and leave positions to the professionals. A clean posture converts the audit from a threat into an appointment.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Case study: HealthTrade's compliance year", "13", "<p>The silent partner, professionally held. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The consolidation (Chapters 1, 3).</b> The first month assembles what the earlier modules built: the four custody accounts confirmed and typed; the item masters' tax categories audited — the pharmacy's exempt basic-medicine lines against its standard-rated cosmetics, <b>14 items re-categorised</b>, the mixed-supply registers now able to tell their streams apart, and the apportionment question handed to the accountant <b>with clean data</b>. Then the first full monthly VAT routine, end to end: registers closed, both bridges tied to the kobo, the return prepared from the system, filed, remitted from the new reserve, VAT Payable verified at zero. The accountant's note in the file: <i>first month the return and the ledger were the same document.</i></p><p><b>The certificate chase (Chapter 4).</b> The WHT Receivable archaeology: the sufferer-side register, reconstructed from allocation history, finds <b>₦1.34m of deductions suffered over two years — certificates held for only ₦610k</b>. The chase is instituted: outstanding certificates listed per customer, aged, worked like receivables — the corporate customers' finance teams contacted with the professional ask; the government customer escalated through its due process. Eight months later, certificates for a further <b>₦590k</b> are recovered and handed to the accountant for the CIT credit: the business's own tax, un-paid-twice, at the cost of a standing worklist. The remaining <b>₦140k</b> — deductions by two defunct customers, certificates unobtainable — is written to the accountant's judgment with its file: even the loss is a documented decision instead of a quiet leak.</p><p><b>The reserve's first test (Chapter 7).</b> Month four: a heavy import quarter squeezes cash, and the old instinct surfaces in a management meeting — <i>the VAT account has ₦2.1m sitting idle; borrow it for the Emzor payment, replace it by the 21st.</i> The custody argument is restated — the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default — and the <b>alternative is worked instead</b>: the receivables push accelerates two collections, and the stock ladder releases a slow line. The reserve stays whole; remittance day passes as a transfer, as designed. The MD's conversion is complete by Q3, when the reserve sweep becomes the month's first standing transfer rather than its last argument.</p><p><b>The desk query (Chapter 8).</b> Month nine brings the authority's automated mismatch letter — for the <b>pre-system</b> period, where a parallel spreadsheet had filed what the old records could not reproduce. The reply is built the on-record way: the current months demonstrated (register, return, remittance, zero — one folder handed over per month), the legacy divergence quantified and explained, the accountant leading the position. The query closes with a small legacy settlement and a sentence in the closing letter that files itself as the module's testimonial: current periods reconcile to source.</i></p>"),
L("Chapter 10 — Common mistakes & the fifth law", "11", "<p>The tax layer's recurring failures — each one a business somewhere is living right now — then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — custody spent.</b> Symptom: remittance day as recurring crisis; the VAT account funded by scramble; interest and penalties as line items. Cause: the state's money used as float (Chapters 1, 7) — the most expensive credit line in Nigeria, taken by default. Fix: the reserve account with its sweep rhythm, the working-capital problem solved honestly with the real tools, and the custody framing taught until it holds.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the parallel computation.</b> Symptom: returns prepared in spreadsheets beside the system; filed numbers and booked numbers diverging; nobody able to say which is true. Cause: two sources (Chapter 6) — the return no longer restates the ledger. Fix: the return from the system, the register bridges as the pre-filing gate, and the spreadsheet retired to what it should be — a formatting tool, never a source.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — the unreconciled registers.</b> Symptom: register totals and account movements drifting apart unexamined; the desk review's mismatch letter arriving as a surprise. Cause: the bridges skipped (Chapters 3, 6) — while the authority's e-invoice rails now run their half automatically. Fix: the register-to-account line in every monthly routine, divergences worked before filing, and the zeroing invariant verified as each cycle's close.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — certificates uncollected.</b> Symptom: WHT suffered for years with a certificate file that fits in an envelope; the CIT bill paid gross. Cause: the sufferer side run on resignation (Chapter 4) — the business's own tax paid twice, quietly. Fix: the deduction captured at allocation, the chase as a standing aged worklist, and the recovered credits handed to the accountant with their evidence — the module's most reliably self-funding routine.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — payroll beside the system.</b> Symptom: salaries in a spreadsheet, a monthly lump journal, PAYE computed elsewhere, state splits improvised, the annual return an archaeology project. Cause: the register that can produce any slice replaced by one that produces none (Chapter 5). Fix: payroll into the system (or rigorously bridged), the custody chain checked monthly, payslips as the standing employee certificate, and the annual return reduced to an extract.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — penalties as a cost of doing business.</b> Symptom: late-filing and late-remittance penalties absorbed without comment, month after month. Cause: no calendar, no owners, no reserve — the whole module absent. Fix: the calendar built with named owners, the reserve funded, and the penalty line watched to zero: it is the single clearest gauge of whether this module is installed.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: the custody idea and the wallet's compartments; the Nigerian tax set with the federal/state split and the 2025 reform's three working consequences; the VAT net position, the two bridges, and the three classifications with the mixed-supply rule; both WHT directions with the ₦1m worked shape, the certificate obligations, and the chase; the payroll custody family, the tie chain, and the payslip rule; the six-step routine and the zeroing invariant's four stories; the calendar's shape and the reserve discipline; and the audit posture with its three encounter types.</p><p><b>The fifth law: tax money is held, not owned — reconciled to its registers, filed from the system, remitted from the reserve, and zeroed every cycle.</b> Module 6 turns to the address system: cost centers and dimensions.</p>"),
]

