# ============================ ACCOUNTS 6 ============================
LESSONS_A6 = [
L("Chapter 1 — Profit has an address", "11", "<p>Module 1 planted the rule in one sentence — the chart answers <b>what kind</b> of money; dimensions answer <b>whose and where</b> — and deferred the machinery here. This module is that machinery: how one business reads many profits — per branch, per service line, per project — without the chart ever learning their names.</p><p><b>The question this module answers.</b> Every owner of a multi-branch business asks it, usually in the first meeting: <i>which branch actually makes money?</i> The unstructured answer is folklore — the flagship \"carries\" the network, the new branch \"must be losing\", the service side \"pays for itself\" — all asserted, none known. The structured answer requires that every naira of revenue and cost carries, from the moment it posts, an <b>address</b>: the branch, the line, the project it belongs to. Then the P&amp;L can be filtered by address and read true. That address is the <b>dimension</b>, and the module's design rule governs everything in it: <b>one chart, many lenses</b> — the chart stays a single language (Rent is one account, everywhere), and the dimensions multiply the readings of it (Rent at Ibadan, Rent at Akure) without multiplying the words.</p><p><b>The disease this module prevents.</b> The alternative design, met constantly: the per-branch chart copy — <i>Rent - Ibadan</i>, <i>Rent - Akure</i>, <i>Rent - HQ</i>, and so on for every expense and every branch. Three branches turn a 60-line P&amp;L structure into 180 accounts; five branches into 300. Every new branch becomes chart surgery, every firm-wide read a manual re-addition of scattered leaves, and every posting a navigation exercise through near-identical names, with the mis-post rate rising with the count. The cure: the branch belongs in the <b>dimension field</b>, entered once, cascading by default (Chapter 3), and the chart never grows when the business does. The test of the design is exactly that: <b>opening branch four should touch the dimension list and not one account.</b></p><p><b>What dimensions are not.</b> Three boundary stakes before the machinery:</p><p>• <b>Not a second chart.</b> Dimensions carry no balances of their own — they are tags on GL entries, and every dimensional read is the same GL, filtered.<br>• <b>Not only for the P&amp;L</b> — balance-sheet dimensioning exists and has uses — but the P&amp;L is where the payoff lives and where this module works.<br>• <b>Not free.</b> Every mandatory dimension taxes every posting forever, which is why Chapter 7 guards the door on adding more.</p><p><b>Module contents.</b> Chapter 2: the cost center tree. Chapter 3: the wiring — defaults, cascade, validation. Chapter 4: the branch P&amp;L and how to read it honestly. Chapter 5: allocating shared costs. Chapter 6: budgets against actuals. Chapter 7: the second dimension — service lines and projects. Chapter 8: HealthTrade's addresses. Chapter 9: review and the law.</p>"),
L("Chapter 2 — Cost centers: the address book", "12", "<p>The primary dimension has a name and a structure: the <b>cost center</b> — and its design follows the same tree rules as the warehouse tree and the chart.</p><p><b>The tree.</b> Cost centers form a tree: <b>group</b> cost centers for rollup reading (Branches; Operations), and <b>leaf</b> cost centers that take postings (Ibadan Branch; Akure Branch; Head Office). Postings to groups are refused by design — the third appearance of the rule: money exists at leaves; groups exist so leaves can be read together. The company's tree roots at a main cost center that anchors the whole. Every earlier tree discipline transfers untouched: named for the decade, shallow (two levels below root serves almost every trading business), designed from what must be read, changed as governed events, retired rather than deleted.</p><p><b>Designing the address book.</b> The design question is <b>whose performance the business manages</b> — the accountability structure, not the org chart's vanity:</p><p>• A leaf per unit that has an <b>owner and a monthly P&amp;L conversation</b>: each branch (a manager answers for it); head office (the shared-function home Chapter 5 allocates from); the warehouse or distribution operation where it is managed as its own unit.<br>• <b>Not</b> a leaf per paper department, per team of two, or per activity nobody reviews separately. The reading test again: a cost center that nobody's monthly conversation names is sprawl wearing a lanyard.</p><p>A shortcut that makes design fast: the cost center tree usually <b>shadows the warehouse tree's accountability layer</b> — the people who answer for stock are frequently the people who answer for the P&amp;L. Where the two trees diverge, the divergence should be explainable in one sentence, or it is a mistake in one tree or the other.</p><p><b>The default machinery.</b> Three layers carry the addressing labour:</p><p>1. <b>The company default</b> — the unaddressed posting's fallback. Chapter 3 argues this fallback should almost never fire.<br>2. <b>Master defaults</b> — the warehouse or branch record wired to its cost center; item groups where product-line reading matters; and…<br>3. <b>User defaults</b> — where the operator <b>is</b> the address: the till assistant whose every posting belongs to their branch.</p><p>The design goal: a well-wired business addresses <b>ninety-something percent of its postings without anyone touching the field</b>. The address is written by the setup, not by discipline — because discipline decays and defaults don't.</p><p>Practical rule: keep the leaf count honest. Each leaf is a monthly page someone must own and read; a tree with more leaves than owners is already telling you which ones to merge.</p>"),
L("Chapter 3 — Wiring the address into every posting", "13", "<p>An address book is useless if letters go unaddressed. This chapter is the wiring: where the dimension gets written, how defaults cascade, and the validation that makes the address a rule instead of a hope.</p><p><b>Where the address is written.</b> Every P&amp;L-touching document carries the field, at two levels:</p><p>• <b>Header level</b> — the whole document one address: the branch invoice, the branch expense.<br>• <b>Row level</b> — each line its own address: the head-office purchase that splits across branches, the invoice mixing service lines. <b>Row overrides header</b>, because real documents mix addresses more often than design imagines.</p><p>The posting map (module 1) gains its dimension column: the sales invoice's income line carries the selling branch (cascaded from the till, the warehouse, or the user); the delivery's COGS carries the same (the warehouse-to-cost-center wiring doing the work); the purchase invoice's expense lines carry their consumers (row-level where consumption splits); the journal's rows carry theirs (module 2 already demanded it — an expense journal that charges without addressing is a letter posted blank); and payroll carries each employee's unit.</p><p><b>The cascade.</b> Defaults resolve in a fixed order — <b>the specific beats the general</b>:</p><p>1. The row's explicit entry, over<br>2. The header's, over<br>3. The master-derived default (item group, warehouse, customer where wired), over<br>4. The user's default, over<br>5. The company fallback.</p><p>The wiring hour sets this cascade so the front line almost never sees the field: the Ibadan till user's sales land at Ibadan because the user and the warehouse both say so; the Akure expense claim lands at Akure because the claimant does. The field surfaces for human decision only where the machine genuinely cannot know — the shared purchase, the split service — which is exactly where a human should decide.</p><p><b>Validation: the address as a rule.</b> The dimension-validation settings close the gap between mostly-addressed and always-addressed: dimensions marked <b>mandatory for the account ranges that need them</b> — all income and expense accounts, at minimum — so the P&amp;L posting that cannot save without its address never posts blank. The company fallback then catches only what validation deliberately exempts, and the monthly test is simple: <b>the unaddressed lump at the default cost center should be at or near zero.</b> A growing lump means a gap in the cascade or an exemption that should not exist — swept to true addresses while small, and the gap fixed at its source.</p><p>Practical rule: verify the wiring the same way the posting map was verified — one live document per kind, the addresses read aloud. An hour at setup buys a P&amp;L that filters true from month one.</p>"),
L("Chapter 4 — The branch P&L: the payoff read", "13", "<p>The addresses exist so this page can: the branch P&amp;L — the owner's first-meeting question, answered monthly and honestly. The read has two halves, and keeping them separate is the chapter's whole discipline.</p><p><b>The natural half: revenue and direct cost.</b> Filtered by the branch's cost center, the P&amp;L's top assembles itself from the wiring: revenue by branch (every sale carried its source), COGS by branch (the delivery's valuation release carried it too), and the branch's <b>direct</b> expenses — its rent, its payroll, its power, its local running costs, each posted with its address at entry. This much is arithmetic, and its honesty is exactly the wiring's honesty — which is why the unaddressed-lump sweep precedes every monthly read.</p><p><b>The harder half: shared costs.</b> Head office exists — the MD's salary, the accountant, the shared warehouse, group marketing, the system's own subscription — and none of it posted to a branch, because none of it belongs to one. Should it appear on branch P&amp;Ls at all? Chapter 5 holds the allocation rules; this chapter holds the reading frame that makes them usable: <b>contribution before net</b>.</p><p>• <b>The contribution view</b> — branch revenue minus branch COGS minus branch direct costs — answers the operational question: does this branch, as a trading unit, generate surplus? Every number on the page is a <b>fact</b>: posted where it happened, nothing estimated, nothing arguable. This is the <b>branch manager's page</b> — they control everything on it.<br>• <b>The fully-loaded view</b> — contribution minus an allocated share of head office — answers a different question: does the branch cover its share of existing overheads? Its numbers are part fact, part <b>policy</b> (the allocation basis is a choice). This is the <b>owner's portfolio page</b> — worth seeing, dangerous to act on alone.</p><p>Keep the two views separate and labelled, always.</p><p><b>The classic misread, pre-empted.</b> The fully-loaded view's danger closes branches that should stay open. The newest or smallest branch shows a fully-loaded loss — its allocated overhead share swamps its young contribution — and someone proposes closing it. Walk the arithmetic before agreeing: close the branch and its contribution <b>leaves</b> the network, while the allocated overhead <b>stays</b> and re-lands on the surviving branches. The business is poorer by the contribution, and the loaded view now frowns at the next-smallest branch. The rule that prevents the spiral: <b>open, close, and expand on contribution; use the loaded view only to size the overhead conversation.</b> Chapter 8 shows the rule earning its keep at HealthTrade's Akure branch.</p>"),
L("Chapter 5 — Allocating shared costs", "12", "<p>The costs that genuinely serve everyone must either stay unallocated — honest, but the loaded view stays unbuilt — or be shared by rule. The allocation rules: brief, written, and deliberately boring.</p><p><b>Direct first, always.</b> The first rule shrinks the problem before sharing it: everything attributable at source is posted there and never enters allocation. The group marketing campaign that ran for one branch's opening is that branch's. The head-office staffer seconded to a project is the project's. The shared warehouse's costs, where the warehouse serves branches measurably, can be recharged by throughput if the business wants that precision. Every naira attributed directly is a naira the allocation argument never touches — mature businesses allocate a <b>residue, not a bloc</b>: the smaller the allocated pool, the smaller the fiction, and the quieter the arguments.</p><p><b>Bases for the residue.</b> What remains — genuinely joint costs — shares by a <b>written basis</b>, chosen per cost family:</p><p>• <b>Revenue share</b> — the default for general overheads: simple, proportional to activity.<br>• <b>Headcount</b> — for people-driven costs: HR, welfare, training.<br>• <b>Space</b> — for premises-driven costs where premises are shared.<br>• <b>Throughput</b> — for the distribution recharge, where measured.</p><p>The specific numbers matter less than three properties. The basis is <b>written</b> — on the policy page, so every allocation question is answered by pointing, not arguing. It is <b>stable</b> — changed annually at most, as an event with a memo; a basis that moves monthly is a dial, and movements should mean something. And it is <b>visible</b> — allocated lines shown <b>as</b> allocated on the loaded view: labelled, separable, never blended into direct costs, so any reader can reconstruct contribution by covering one section with a thumb.</p><p><b>The allocation wars, and their treaty.</b> Where allocation is argued monthly, the pattern is always the same three failures: the basis is unwritten or unstable; the allocated pool is too large (direct-first neglected); and the loaded view is being used for decisions it cannot carry — <b>branch bonuses on loaded profit is the classic accelerant</b>, converting an accounting convention into a monthly salary dispute. The treaty reverses all three: direct-first shrinks the pool, the written basis freezes annually, the lines stay labelled — and incentives move to <b>contribution</b>, the page the manager actually controls.</p><p>Practical rule: when an allocation argument starts, do not defend the number — point at the page. If the page cannot settle it, the page is what needs fixing, once, rather than the argument monthly.</p>"),
L("Chapter 6 — Budgets vs actuals: the forward address", "12", "<p>Dimensions address the past; budgets address the future — and the machinery joins them: the budget set per address, the actuals landing beside it, and the monthly variance read that turns the pair into management.</p><p><b>The budget machinery.</b> Budgets attach where the reading lives: per <b>cost center</b> (the branch's expense budget; head office's own), per <b>account</b> or account group, per <b>fiscal year</b> with a monthly or periodic distribution — the even split for steady costs, the seasonal curve for the trade that has one (the December-heavy retail budget expects its own Q4). The system holds the budget beside the actuals it accumulates, and the variance — actual against budget, by address, by account, monthly and cumulative — becomes a standing read instead of a spreadsheet reunion.</p><p><b>Budget actions.</b> Where wanted, the machinery acts on exceeding: <b>warn</b>, or <b>stop</b>. The hard stop is reserved for the account families where overspend is a governance failure rather than a trading reality — a stop on COGS in a good sales month is the machinery fighting the business. Warn widely; stop narrowly.</p><p><b>Building budgets that mean something.</b> Budget quality is structural, not aspirational:</p><p>1. <b>Built per address from that address's own history</b> — the branch's last-year actuals as the base, which the address system supplies by construction: the wiring's first forward dividend.<br>2. <b>Adjusted by known changes</b> — the new branch's ramp, the rent review, the headcount plan.<br>3. <b>Owned by the address's owner</b> — the branch manager who built their number defends it differently than one who received it; the budget conversation is the accountability structure exercising.<br>4. <b>Approved as a set</b> — the rollup must reconcile to the business's plan. Branch ambitions summing to more than the network's cash is a conversation for November, not a discovery in March.</p><p><b>The variance read.</b> Monthly, by address, with one discipline: <b>cause before comment</b>. A variance decomposes — volume versus rate (sold more units, or spent more per unit); timing versus level (the cost that moved months, or the cost that grew); one-off versus run-rate (the repair that happened, or the drift that will repeat) — and the read's output is the standard shape: the material variances, each carrying a one-line story and, where needed, an action with an owner. Thresholds keep the read short: variances below the line are noise; the read exists for the stories.</p><p>Practical rule: a budget cited only in January and December is decoration, and a budget used mainly for blame teaches managers to pad numbers and hide news. The monthly read, seated in the close, causes before comments, is what keeps the instrument alive.</p>"),
L("Chapter 7 — Projects and service lines: the second dimension", "12", "<p>The cost center answered <b>where</b>; some businesses need a second question answered — <b>what line, which job</b> — and the accounting-dimension machinery carries it. The second lens, and the guard that keeps lenses from multiplying into fog.</p><p><b>When a dimension earns existence.</b> The reading test at its sternest, because every mandatory dimension taxes every posting forever. A second dimension earns its field when <b>all three</b> hold:</p><p>1. Someone will <b>read its cut monthly</b> and act on it.<br>2. The cut <b>crosses the first dimension</b> — the service line that exists at several branches. If it nests inside one branch, the cost center tree already carries it.<br>3. The postings can be <b>attributed at entry</b> with reasonable defaults — a dimension the front line cannot fill truthfully fills falsely; the address system's honesty is only ever the entry moment's honesty.</p><p><b>Two dimensions serve almost every trading business that needs any; the third is rare and argued hard; the fourth is fog.</b></p><p><b>The service-line lens.</b> The mixed business's standing need — the pharmacy with a clinic corner, the retailer with an installation arm, the distributor with a logistics-for-hire side: one branch, two businesses, and the branch P&amp;L alone cannot say which one earns. The service-line dimension cuts the same GL the other way: revenue by line (the invoice rows carry it — item-group defaults doing the cascade: clinic services default to Clinic, shop items to Retail), direct costs by line (the clinic's pharmacist, its consumables), and the two-way read the pair of dimensions unlocks — the <b>branch-by-line matrix</b> (Ibadan's clinic against Akure's clinic; each branch's retail core). That matrix answers expansion's real question: not <i>should we add clinics</i>, but <i>the clinic line contributes X at Ibadan and Y at Akure — what differs, and which model travels?</i> Contribution framing governs exactly as in Chapter 4, and shared-within-branch costs (the premises both lines sit in) allocate by Chapter 5's rules or, better, stay at the branch's shared level unallocated.</p><p><b>The project lens.</b> Job-shaped work — the fit-out contract, the installation project, the one-off supply tender — wants revenue, costs, and margin <b>per job</b>. The project dimension carries it, with the one property branches and lines don't have: a <b>lifecycle</b>. Projects open when the job opens and close when it closes — opened with a code and an owner, posted to while live, read at completion (the job's margin against its quote: the estimating feedback loop), and closed so the value list stays short and current. A project list full of ancient open codes is the dimension decaying into noise.</p><p>Practical rule: before adding any dimension, write the monthly read it will serve and name its reader. No named reader, no dimension.</p>"),
L("Chapter 8 — Case study: HealthTrade's addresses", "13", "<p>The address system, installed and read. Every chapter appears.</p><p><b>The tree and the wiring (Chapters 2-3).</b> The design hour with the MD and the accountant produces four leaves under two groups — <b>Branches</b> (Central/Lagos, Ibadan, Akure) and <b>Head Office</b> — shadowing the warehouse tree's accountability layer exactly. The one divergence gets its one-sentence justification: the shared distribution operation takes its own leaf, because it is managed as a unit and will be read as one. Defaults cascade from warehouses and users; income and expense accounts get mandatory validation; and the posting map is re-verified with its new dimension column — one live document per kind, addresses read aloud. Month one's unaddressed lump: <b>₦0</b> — the validation refused every blank at birth.</p><p><b>The first branch P&amp;L, and the MD's lesson (Chapter 4).</b> Month two's read, contribution format, three branches side by side: Central contributes strongly, Ibadan solidly — and Akure, eight months old, contributes <b>₦310,000</b>… which the fully-loaded view (head office allocated by revenue share) turns into a <b>₦140,000 \"loss\"</b>. The MD's first instinct is the classic — <i>Akure isn't working</i> — and the arithmetic is walked at the whiteboard: close Akure and its ₦310k contribution leaves the network; the allocated overhead stays and re-lands on Central and Ibadan; the business is ₦310k a month poorer, and the loaded view now frowns at Ibadan. The lesson lands as the rule — <b>close on contribution, expand on contribution</b> — and Akure, read rightly, is a young branch working, not a loss. The loaded view keeps its one real job: it sizes the head-office conversation, which produces one genuine action — the group's software and admin stack is renegotiated, head office lightens <b>8%</b>, and every branch's loaded view improves without a single allocation argument.</p><p><b>The policy settles an argument (Chapter 5).</b> Month four: the Ibadan manager disputes the marketing allocation — a campaign <i>that only helped Lagos</i>. The policy page answers by pointing: campaign costs attributable to a branch post <b>direct</b> — and this one, on inspection, was indeed Lagos-specific, mis-posted to the shared pool and corrected by the rules to Central's direct costs. The residue keeps sharing by the written basis. The argument dies in one meeting because the page, not the loudest voice, decides.</p><p><b>Budgets arrive (Chapter 6).</b> Year two's budgets are built per branch from each branch's own year-one actuals, adjusted for known changes (Akure's ramp, the Central rent review), owned by the managers who built them, and approved as a reconciling set. The monthly variance read seats itself in the close: causes before comments, thresholds keeping it short — and February's first real story (Ibadan's power spend up 40%: a generator fault, one-off, fixed) is discussed in week one instead of discovered in an argument at year-end.</p>"),
L("Chapter 9 — Common mistakes & the sixth law", "11", "<p>The address layer's recurring failures, then the review list.</p><p><b>Pattern 1 — the per-branch chart copy.</b> Symptom: Rent-Ibadan, Rent-Akure, Rent-HQ… hundreds of accounts, chart surgery at every opening, firm-wide reads assembled by hand. Cause: the address written into the language (Chapter 1) — dimensions unknown or distrusted at setup. Fix: the migration — dimensions wired, the copies merged to single accounts by documented entries with history preserved, validation installed, the chart returned to one language. Priced honestly as the archaeology it is.</p><p><b>Pattern 2 — the unaddressed lump.</b> Symptom: a default cost center absorbing a growing share of the P&amp;L; branch reads everyone quietly distrusts. Cause: the optional address decaying (Chapter 3) — blanks accumulating where validation should have refused them. Fix: the lump swept to true addresses while small, mandatory validation on the P&amp;L ranges, the cascade tightened so the field almost never surfaces — and the monthly sweep kept as the standing hygiene line before every branch read.</p><p><b>Pattern 3 — allocation wars.</b> Symptom: monthly arguments about shared-cost shares; bonuses fought over loaded profit; the basis renegotiated by whoever shouts. Cause: the rules absent (Chapter 5) — pool too large, basis unwritten, the loaded view overloaded with decisions it cannot carry. Fix: direct-first shrinking the pool, the written basis frozen annually, labelled and separable lines, and incentives moved to contribution.</p><p><b>Pattern 4 — the death-spiral close.</b> Symptom: the smallest branch closed on its loaded loss; the network poorer; the next branch now \"losing\". Cause: allocation-led decisions (Chapter 4) — the misread institutionalised. Fix: the arithmetic taught before it is needed, contribution governing open/close/expand calls, and the loaded view returned to its one job: sizing the overhead conversation.</p><p><b>Pattern 5 — budget theatre.</b> Symptom: budgets built in January, cited in December, absent in between; managers budgeting slack and hiding news. Cause: the instrument as decoration, or as punishment (Chapter 6) — either kills the conversation it exists to create. Fix: the monthly variance read seated in the close, thresholds that surface stories rather than noise, causes before comments — and the culture reset: variances are for discussing early, not punishing late.</p><p><b>Pattern 6 — dimension fog.</b> Symptom: four mandatory dimensions; the front line inventing values to save documents; every read doubted. Cause: lenses added without the three-part earning test (Chapter 7). Fix: the test applied retroactively — dimensions nobody reads retired, attribution honesty restored at entry, and the discipline going forward: no named reader, no dimension.</p><p><b>The review list.</b> Confirm you can state: the one-chart-many-lenses rule and the branch-four test; the cost center tree's design rules and the default layers; the cascade order and the mandatory-validation rule with the zero-lump test; contribution versus fully-loaded and the close-on-contribution rule with its arithmetic; the allocation rules — direct first, written stable visible bases, and the treaty; budget quality's four structural properties and cause-before-comment; and the second dimension's three-part earning test with the service-line matrix and the project lifecycle.</p><p><b>The sixth law: one chart, many lenses — every posting carries its address, contribution decides, and allocation is written.</b> Module 7 assembles the period close and the financial statements.</p>"),
]

